rted index/topic/qrels combination into a UI-driven evaluation workflow, while still relying on actual Anserini commands underneath.\n\n## Goals\n- Drive catalog discovery, retrieval, and evaluation through the Anserini fatjar CLI.\n- Expose Anserini's available prebuilt Lucene inverted indexes through the app.\n- Discover or surface compatible topics and qrels/evaluation resources for indexes that can be evaluated.\n- Treat CACM as the default small end-to-end evaluation dataset.\n- Offer a metric selector that includes modern ranking metrics such as `nDCG@10` and `Recall@1000` when the selected dataset supports them.\n- Display the evaluation score and supporting metadata that lets a user verify what was executed.\n- Ship a Playwright end-to-end browser test that demonstrates the app actually performs retrieval and evaluation.\n\n## Non-Goals\n- Using the Anserini REST API.\n- Implementing a custom retrieval engine.\n- Tuning BM25 parameters.\n- Automated hyperparameter tuning or grid search.\n- Training or fine-tuning models.\n- Defaulting to large-scale evaluation.\n- Authentication or user account management.\n- Production deployment infrastructure.\n\n## Users\n- Information-retrieval researchers exploring Anserini's prebuilt retrieval resources.\n- Developers validating Anserini search and evaluation workflows.\n- Demo users who want a concrete local interface for running evaluations.\n\n## Core Requirements\n- Treat the repo-local Anserini skills as authoritative references: `install-anserini-fatjar`, `anserini-cli`, and `anserini-reproduction`.\n- Begin by installing or locating the Anserini fatjar through `install-anserini-fatjar` and verify that it functions.\n- Use Anserini command-line tools for registry discovery, retrieval, and evaluation. Do not use the Anserini REST API for this task.\n- Do not hardcode or mock retrieval results, run files, evaluation scores, or catalog data.\n- Derive the exact Anserini CLI syntax from the repo-local skills and `command help` output rather than guessing.\n- Build a local web application with a browser UI.\n- Discover Anserini's prebuilt Lucene inverted indexes from the Anserini prebuilt-index registry rather than relying on a hardcoded index list alone.\n- Provide a searchable or filterable catalog view for the available prebuilt Lucene inverted indexes.\n- Display useful catalog metadata for each index when available, including name, type, description, and whether the app considers it evaluable.\n- Discover or expose topic sets from Anserini's topics registry.\n- Pair indexes with compatible topics and qrels/evaluation resources where this can be determined from Anserini registries, repo-local skills, or reproduction docs.\n- Visually distinguish indexes"},"partialJson":"{\"path\": \"/Users/lily/dev/pi-bench/experiments/evaluator/minimax-workspace/.pi-bench-rewrites/PRD.md\", \"content\": \"# PRD (rewritten restatement)\\n\\n## Title\\nAnserini Prebuilt Index Evaluator\\n\\n## Summary\\nConstruct a locally hosted web application that surfaces Anserini's prebuilt Lucene inverted indexes and runs reproducible retrieval evaluations against them. The application is intended to make the index catalog browsable, automatically pair indexes with compatible topics and qrels where the registries support it, drive Anserini retrieval and evaluation through the fatjar CLI, and display the resulting score along with the generated run artifacts.\\n\\n## Problem\\nAnserini provides registries for prebuilt indexes, topics, and evaluation resources, but working with them today requires moving between command-line invocations, registry output, run files, and evaluator output. A lightweight local application is needed that exposes the catalog in a browser and turns a supported index/topic/qrels combination into a UI-driven evaluation workflow, while still relying on actual Anserini commands underneath.\\n\\n## Goals\\n- Drive catalog discovery, retrieval, and evaluation through the Anserini fatjar CLI.\\n- Expose Anserini's available prebuilt Lucene inverted indexes through the app.\\n- Discover or surface compatible topics and qrels/evaluation resources for indexes that can be evaluated.\\n- Treat CACM as the default small end-to-end evaluation dataset.\\n- Offer a metric selector that includes modern ranking metrics such as `nDCG@10` and `Recall@1000` when the selected dataset supports them.\\n- Display the evaluation score and supporting metadata that lets a user verify what was executed.\\n- Ship a Playwright end-to-end browser test that demonstrates the app actually performs retrieval and evaluation.\\n\\n## Non-Goals\\n- Using the Anserini REST API.\\n- Implementing a custom retrieval engine.\\n- Tuning BM25 parameters.\\n- Automated hyperparameter tuning or grid search.\\n- Training or fine-tuning models.\\n- Defaulting to large-scale evaluation.\\n- Authentication or user account management.\\n- Production deployment infrastructure.\\n\\n## Users\\n- Information-retrieval researchers exploring Anserini's prebuilt retrieval resources.\\n- Developers validating Anserini search and evaluation workflows.\\n- Demo users who want a concrete local interface for running evaluations.\\n\\n## Core Requirements\\n- Treat the repo-local Anserini skills as authoritative references: `install-anserini-fatjar`, `anserini-cli`, and `anserini-reproduction`.\\n- Begin by installing or locating the Anserini fatjar through `install-anserini-fatjar` and verify that it functions.\\n- Use Anserini command-line tools for registry discovery, retrieval, and evaluation. Do not use the Anserini REST API for this task.\\n- Do not hardcode or mock retrieval results, run files, evaluation scores, or catalog data.\\n- Derive the exact Anserini CLI syntax from the repo-local skills and `command help` output rather than guessing.\\n- Build a local web application with a browser UI.\\n- Discover Anserini's prebuilt Lucene inverted indexes from the Anserini prebuilt-index registry rather than relying on a hardcoded index list alone.\\n- Provide a searchable or filterable catalog view for the available prebuilt Lucene inverted indexes.\\n- Display useful catalog metadata for each index when available, including name, type, description, and whether the app considers it evaluable.\\n- Discover or expose topic sets from Anserini's topics registry.\\n- Pair indexes with compatible topics and qrels/evaluation resources where this can be determined from Anserini registries, repo-local skills, or reproduction docs.\\n- Visually distinguish indexes","index":1}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax","model":"MiniMax-M3","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":4909,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":4909,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0.00058908,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00058908}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1780721204130,"responseId":"0672d934531e45780fc4ca93c5ca1cde"}},"message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, the directory is empty. Now let me write the rewritten PRD to that location. I need to:\n- Preserve every requirement, acceptance criterion, constraint, file name, command, dependency, and setup instruction\n- Keep the task scope identical\n- Use my own wording and organization\n- Retain precise technical details\n\nLet me draft the rewritten version. I'll aim for a neutral restatement that keeps all the same content but in my own voice.","thinkingSignature":"2eb9a8e586fc398da471fe9aba89934b82edc3a950edba9ce83de76f294f8769"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_lpmrmm3es2tg_1","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/Users/lily/dev/pi-bench/experiments/evaluator/minimax-workspace/.pi-bench-rewrites/PRD.md","content":"# PRD (rewritten restatement)\n\n## Title\nAnserini Prebuilt Index Evaluator\n\n## Summary\nConstruct a locally hosted web application that surfaces Anserini's prebuilt Lucene inverted indexes and runs reproducible retrieval evaluations against them. The application is intended to 